{-
   Cross-block place forwarding: a field read in one block answered by the write in another.

   What every function here is really asserting is a *disappearance*. The reads go away in
   opt_promote.cpp and then the record goes away in opt_scalar.cpp, so what `.opt.expect` should show
   is an `alloc` that is not there and arithmetic over values that were never in storage - and
   `.js.expect` beside it is where that is easiest to read, since a record on that target is an object
   literal and its absence is unmistakable.

   The numbers are asserted on both targets and against the unoptimized build because the ways this
   goes wrong are ways that change one: a phi taking its alternative from the wrong edge, a read
   answered by a write that does not reach it, a field whose value came from its neighbour.
-}

data Point {x: Int, y: Int}
data Outer {inner: Point, tag: Int}

fn widen(&p: Point) -> {}:
    p.x = p.x + 1

-- The plain case, and the one inlining leaves behind everywhere: a record built in one block and
-- read in another. Nothing merges - the construction dominates both reads - so no phi survives and
-- the whole record is arithmetic on `a`.
fn branched(a: Int) -> Int:
    let p = Point {x: a, y: a + 1}

    if a > 0:
        return p.x + p.y

    return p.y

-- A field written on one path and not the other, which is the case a phi exists for. `p.y` becomes
-- a real merge of 30 and 2; `p.x` is written once above both arms and stays a value.
fn partial(a: Int) -> Int:
    let &p = Point {x: 1, y: 2}

    if a > 0:
        p.y = 30

    return p.x + p.y

-- Two merges of the same field in a row, which is what makes the phis a chain rather than a case:
-- the second one's incoming value is the first one's result on one edge and its own write on the
-- other. `p.y` is untouched throughout and has to survive as the constant it was given.
fn merged(a: Int) -> Int:
    let &p = Point {x: 0, y: 7}

    if a > 0:
        p.x = 10

    if a > 5:
        p.x = 20

    return p.x + p.y

{-
   A record written before a loop and only read inside it.

   This is the assertion behind `computeAvailability` solving from the optimistic end. Whether `p.x`
   is available at the loop header depends on the back edge, and the back edge's answer depends on
   the header - so from the pessimistic end that circle has nothing to break it and the answer
   converges to "unwritten" for a field the loop merely reads, which is exactly the shape an inlined
   constructor in front of a loop produces. Nothing here should be left in storage, and `p.x * p.y`
   should be one constant.
-}
fn invariant(count: Int) -> Int:
    let p = Point {x: 3, y: 4}
    let &total = 0 :: Int
    let &i = 0 :: Int

    while i < count:
        total = total + p.x * p.y
        i = i + 1

    return total

-- A field the loop writes, which is the phi the header carries around the back edge. `acc.y` is
-- invariant and shares the record with it, so the two have to be told apart by their paths rather
-- than by the local they are in.
fn carried(count: Int) -> Int:
    let &acc = Point {x: 0, y: 5}
    let &i = 0 :: Int

    while i < count:
        acc.x = acc.x + acc.y
        i = i + 1

    return acc.x

-- A nested record, read through in another block. The inner record is written into the outer one
-- *whole*, so there is no field write to forward from until `splitAggregateWrite` has turned that
-- into one write per field of the inner record - which it will only do because the outer local is
-- contained and can therefore still disappear. Both records go, not just the outer one.
fn nested(a: Int) -> Int:
    let o = Outer {inner: Point {x: a, y: a + 1}, tag: 5}

    if a > 0:
        return o.inner.x + o.inner.y + o.tag

    return o.tag

-- The decline. `p` is borrowed mutably into a call, so its address left this frame and containment
-- refuses it - which takes the whole local out of the pass, `p.y` included, even though nothing can
-- have written that. Flow-insensitive on purpose: a local borrowed anywhere is reachable everywhere.
fn escaped(a: Int) -> Int:
    let &p = Point {x: a, y: 1}

    if a > 0:
        widen(p)

    return p.x + p.y

{-
   What is deliberately *not* promoted: `total` and `i` above.

   Both are whole locals of scalar type, and a place with no projections is declined - see
   `collectCandidates`. Neither target keeps one in memory to begin with, so the only thing promoting
   it would change is the shape of the emitted code, and on JS it changes it for the worse: a phi
   there is a fresh variable plus a copy on every edge, where the local was one `var` updated in
   place. The loops above are where that shows, and they are why the rule is worth having.
-}

fn main() -> Int =
    branched(2) + partial(1) + merged(1) + invariant(3) + carried(4) + nested(2) + escaped(5)
